SRC/discord -- discord, as built for OS-9/68000===============================================discord reads text on standard input, learns which word follows which,and writes a few paragraphs of new text by walking those chains atrandom, with the occasional `fnord'.  Each paragraph starts with thenroff request `.PP'.  The author is not named; the Makefile says`Released upon the public domain.'  From the `silly' collection of jokeprograms posted to alt.sources in December 1992.ORIG holds discord.c and its Makefile as posted.What changed in discord.c, and why:  - The nine forward declarations are written without their argument    lists, and the nine function definitions are written in K&R form.    Microware C is K&R and takes no prototypes; ansi2knr (the build    driver's KNR pass) does not recognise these headers, because each    has a blank line before its opening brace.  - `char *strdup();' is declared.  strdup() itself is linked from    SRC/unixlib/strdup.c; it is not in this C library.random() and srandom() are mapped to rand() and srand() by the recipe(-Drandom=rand -Dsrandom=srand), with rand() from /dd/LIB/rand.r.    discord < /dd/DOC/README-KEEP